<Base title="Methodology — Islamic Tech Guide"><article class="prose panel doc-page"><div class="eyebrow">Editorial system</div><h1>Methodology</h1><p>The guide is both an archive and an analysis layer: it records what exists even in crowded categories, evaluates resources progressively, and documents gaps.</p><h2>One source of truth</h2><p>Each Resource is entered once. Cards, tables and detail pages are presentation views over the same record. No duplicate “table version” and “detail version” exists.</p><h2>Review levels</h2><ul><li><strong>Indexed:</strong> identity and core metadata verified.</li><li><strong>Reviewed:</strong> lightweight editorial assessment with evidence.</li><li><strong>Deep review:</strong> deeper comparison, testing and richer evidence.</li></ul><h2 id="aggregators">Aggregators</h2><p>An aggregated repository or random link list is a <strong>Discovery Source</strong>, not a Resource just because it exists. Candidates are extracted and independently verified.</p><p><a href={withBase('/sources/')}>Open the discovery source registry →</a></p><h2>Evidence vs discovery provenance</h2><p>Discovery provenance records where we found a Resource. Evidence supports facts and editorial assessments.</p><h2>Gap analysis</h2><p>A gap can mean absence, weak quality, poor maintenance, platform imbalance, language deficiency, missing developer infrastructure, missing integrations, trust issues or missing standards. v0.1 uses editorial judgment; mandatory scoring is deferred.</p><h2>Fact vs opinion</h2><p>The model is future-ready for claim-level facts vs editorial judgments, but this is not mandatory now.</p><h2>Language</h2><p>The interface is English-first and LTR-first. Translatable content can optionally carry Arabic and render it RTL.</p></article></Base>
